<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 54 0 R
/E (Time lags: Infrastructure investments \(like 5G\) take years to show a return on investment. Supply-side costs: Semiconductor shortages and global hardware costs raise capital expenditure. Uneven effects: Urban users receive 5G benefits much faster than rural users.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
146 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 54 0 R
/E (Trade-offs: Raising prices to pay off debt can lead to short-term subscriber churn, as seen with Vodafone Idea \(Vi\).)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
147 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 54 0 R
/E (Imagine a rural consumer who uses mobile data for agricultural market rates and education. If the telecom oligopoly \(Jio and Airtel\) tacitly colludes to raise base tariffs by 15%, the consumer's monthly expense increases. Because the demand for data is inelastic, the consumer does not disconnect but reduces spending in other areas of their household budget.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
148 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 54 0 R
/E (Conversely, an urban enterprise user might gladly accept a 20% price hike if it is bundled with enterprise-grade 5G security and IoT capabilities. The final result of a pricing strategy depends on incomes, digital dependence, and competition \(or lack thereof\).)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
149 0 obj
<<
